                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1d70d88c-106f-3c3d-8b43-6b255f8dd429",
                        "entry_id": "d4dfd75a-41c1-3b77-8444-70a12a0454a1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Characteristic of a Whig; liberal."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a537c4ca-0fe4-3981-bfe8-c6f31e8905d8",
                        "entry_id": "d4dfd75a-41c1-3b77-8444-70a12a0454a1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Characterized by a belief in inevitable progress, and tending to evaluate the past by the standards of the present; see Whig history."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "capitalized",
                            "often"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b8c59b2c-3997-46d6-a854-7e8abf21b6c9",
                                "sense_id": "a537c4ca-0fe4-3981-bfe8-c6f31e8905d8",
                                "text": "Near-synonym: presentist"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5783b483-6522-480a-adb4-3c749413f93b",
                                "sense_id": "a537c4ca-0fe4-3981-bfe8-c6f31e8905d8",
                                "text": "Finally, whiggish narratives, strewn with heroes, only hinder understanding of how the world works."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0484fdaf-6866-4c71-af31-468193f173f5",
                                "sense_id": "a537c4ca-0fe4-3981-bfe8-c6f31e8905d8",
                                "text": "Abilafia’s article is a classic example of an old-fashioned “Whiggish” narrative. It claims a uniquely moderate and progressive advance toward the development of British institutions, traced continuously from Magna Carta and isolated from the rages and radicalism of the Continent."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e14e2854-0efc-4ccb-a9ee-da3635459e65",
                                "sense_id": "a537c4ca-0fe4-3981-bfe8-c6f31e8905d8",
                                "text": "Because this whiggish view depends on the expectation of progress, liberal politicians are more suspectible to it than their conservative brethren."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "74aee42f-6baf-49e2-a634-2c7b16e1dfd4",
                                "sense_id": "a537c4ca-0fe4-3981-bfe8-c6f31e8905d8",
                                "text": "Of course, the reason we know all this—the reason the episode keeps turning up in Whiggish histories of technology—is that Plato wrote it down."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
